  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on travel patterns associated with a highly localized, high-demand event (a music festival) and may not fully reflect everyday commuting conditions during a strike.",
      "what": "This study measures the impact of a 2025 public transit strike on travel times during a major summer music festival in Québec City, Canada. Using smartphone tracking data and a difference-in-differences framework, it compares travel times to and from the festival site between 2023 and 2025. The analysis reveals that trips were significantly longer when transit services were paralyzed, with the most severe delays concentrated among travelers originating from the city center and inner suburbs.",
      "why": "It provides direct causal evidence of the travel-time savings generated by public transit, demonstrating that transit systems yield meaningful economic benefits even in medium-sized cities with low transit modal shares."
    }
